e  takoyaki or if somebody asks who ate the  takoyaki the answer might be like I say  got Omata meaning I ate it even though  the object is not mentioned again the  object is obvious from the context or  let's say you ask Mickey where does the  takoyaki go takoyaki on token econo  you'll probably say panna cotta meaning  I ate it even though there's no subject  or object they're obvious from the  context there are also equation all  sentences that contain no verb but they  end in a verbal form a verbal form can  be a noun plus a copula or an adjective  plus a copula if you don't sound like  you ensure that this means Ichiro is a  baseball player here there's no verb  just the subject plus topic marker which  will come back to you later then an elm  plus the copula dot can associate a  lockpick IDUs this means this sushi shop  is expensive here we have the subject  topic marker then an adjective plus a  copula death notice that the copulas are  different dot is the casual form while  deaths is the polite form if we want to  say the second sentence casually we take  away the copula since da is not used  after adjectives only nouns  topic marker and subject marker just now  we saw two special little words flaw and  gah what is the topic marker and goth is  the subject marker what's the difference  they both seem to be placed after the  subject of the sentence right well the  topic marker wha is used when you want  to talk about something that is already  part of the conversation and make it the  topic so that everything else in the  sentence relates to it take this example  tanaka-san my association today this  means mr. Tanaka is a nice person the  use of wah in the sentence means that  mr. Tanaka has already been mentioned in  the conversation with something like the  nuance of speaking of mr. Tanaka he is a  nice person the subject marker got on  the other hand is used when you  introduce something new to the  conversation Killa sensei taught that  this means the te