urned into  verbs by adding the word pseudo meaning  to do for example yo D which means  cuisine and you already see it which  means to make food or to cook kill it up  it means cooperation and kill looks it  it means to cooperate some japanese  sentences also contain katakana usually  to represent guide ie go foreign loan  words that aren't Congo  Camila no sky categorical night this  means I don't know how to use the camera  word-for-word its camera possessive  particle use way subject markers don't  know here we see the word comida written  in katakana then we see the kanji  meaning use followed by Okuda Ghana II  so we know it's a Cunha my pronunciation  then there's kata which means way so sky  kata means way of using then we see the  subject marker or focus marker in this  case marking the object then we see waka  donna e which means I don't know again  this is a Chinese character with Okuda  Ghana there are no Congo in this  sentence the two syllabaries hiragana  and katakana are more or less two  versions of the same script each letter  in either alphabet has an equivalent in  the other and they often look fairly  similar except that katakana tends to be  more angular while hiragana is rounder  why do some hit Agana and katakana pairs  look so similar while others don't  well if you remember from before the  kana syllabaries developed from kanji in  some cases kita ghana and katakana  characters developed from the same  country but in other cases they  developed from different kanji  the Kama syllabaries are quite easy to  learn but as learners of Japanese  inevitably discovered reading a sentence  written entirely in kama is actually  harder than reading a sentence that  contains kanji provided that you know  the kanji  that's because kanji give you an  immediate visual cue of a word so you  understand the meaning more quickly than  if you have to phonetically pronounce  all the kana  as you can see Japanese is a fascinating  multifaceted language in fact t